Columbia River Maritime Museum is a museum of maritime history in the northwest United States, located about ten miles (16 km) southeast of the mouth of the Columbia River in Astoria, Oregon. It has a national reputation for the quality of its exhibits and the scope of its collections and was the first museum in Oregon to meet national accreditation standards.","wpu":"Columbia River Maritime Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Columbia_River_Maritime_Museum_exterior_in_2012.jpg/960px-Columbia_River_Maritime_Museum_exterior_in_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Barby Maritime Center","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":46.189926,"lo":-123.819587,"d":2343,"ad":"Marine Drive"},{"n":"Astoria Column","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":46.18131,"lo":-123.817573,"d":2638,"wp":"en:Astoria Column","wd":"Q4811102","ad":"1 Coxcomb Drive","ds":"The Astoria Column is a tower in the northwest United States, overlooking the mouth of the Columbia River on Coxcomb Hill in Astoria, Oregon. Built in 1926, the concrete and steel structure is part of a 30-acre (12 ha) city park called Astor Park. The 125-foot (38 m)-tall column has a 164-step spiral staircase ascending to an exterior observation deck at the top.","wpu":"Astoria Column","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Astoria_Column_2024.jpg/960px-Astoria_Column_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Time Capsule","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":46.181374,"lo":-123.817401,"d":2648},{"n":"Firefighters Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":46.190438,"lo":-123.811196,"d":2991,"h":"Jun-Sep: Sa 12:00-15:00","w":"https://astoriamuseums.org/","ad":"2968 Marine Drive"},{"n":"Sea Lion Dock","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":46.195824,"lo":-123.803792,"d":3636},{"n":"Goonies \"Mikey's House\"","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":46.192862,"lo":-123.79963,"d":3900,"h":"off","wd":"Q60059405","ad":"368 38th Street"},{"n":"Goonies \"Data’s House\"","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":46.193081,"lo":-123.799626,"d":3904,"ad":"344 38th Street"},{"n":"Astoria Pier Love Locks","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":46.197206,"lo":-123.797978,"d":4106},{"n":"Pier 39","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":46.19631,"lo":-123.797405,"d":4129},{"n":"Hanthorn Cannery Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":46.196946,"lo":-123.797442,"d":4140,"h":"Mo-Su 09:00-18:00","w":"https://canneryworker.org/","ad":"39th Street","f":["free"]},{"n":"Warrenton Historical Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":46.161364,"lo":-123.899296,"d":4883},{"n":"Fort Clatsop Replica","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":46.134164,"lo":-123.878997,"d":6493,"wp":"en:Fort Clatsop","ds":"Fort Clatsop was the encampment of the Lewis and Clark Expedition in the Oregon Country near the mouth of the Columbia River during the winter of 1805–1806. Located along the Lewis and Clark River at the north end of the Clatsop Plains approximately 5 miles (8.0 km) southwest of Astoria, the fort was the last encampment of the Corps of Discovery, before embarking on their return trip east to St. Louis.","wpu":"Fort Clatsop","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Fort_Clatsop_replica_2007.jpg/960px-Fort_Clatsop_replica_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Clatsop Interperitive Center","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":46.134441,"lo":-123.880198,"d":6497,"w":"https://www.nps.gov/lewi/index.htm","ph":"+1 503 861 2471","ad":"92343 Fort Clatsop Road"},{"n":"Astoria Wauna Federal Credit Union","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":46.189796,"lo":-123.847404,"d":218,"h":"09:00-17:30","w":"https://waunafcu.org/","ph":"+1 503 3251044","ad":"100 Columbia Avenue"},{"n":"TLC","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":46.190449,"lo":-123.844433,"d":457},{"n":"Wauna Credit Union","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":46.190345,"lo":-123.837149,"d":1000},{"n":"Astoria Post Office","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":46.189401,"lo":-123.835214,"d":1139,"w":"https://tools.usps.com/locations/details/1353543","ad":"750 Commercial Street"},{"n":"Clatsop County Public Services","g":"essentials","k":"wifi","la":46.187928,"lo":-123.834549,"d":1195,"ph":"+1 503 3251000","ad":"800 Exchange Street","wifi":1},{"n":"Astoria Cleaners","g":"essentials","k":"laundry","la":46.178274,"lo":-123.853944,"d":1230,"h":"Mo-Su 07:00-20:00","wifi":1},{"n":"U.S. Bank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":46.188152,"lo":-123.833068,"d":1306,"h":"Mo-Th 09:30-17:00, Fr 09:30-18:00, Sa-Su off","ad":"987 Duane Street"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":46.189148,"lo":-123.832955,"d":1312},{"n":"Maps Credit Union","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":46.18918,"lo":-123.832907,"d":1315,"f":["wc"]},{"n":"Astoria Public Library","g":"essentials","k":"library","la":46.188136,"lo":-123.832473,"d":1352,"wd":"Q69771322","ad":"450 10th Street","wifi":1},{"n":"Astoria Downtown Market","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":46.189262,"lo":-123.832405,"d":1354,"h":"Mo-Su 08:00-22:00","ad":"1020 Commercial Street"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":46.188946,"lo":-123.832151,"d":1373},{"n":"Wells Fargo","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":46.189222,"lo":-123.829883,"d":1548,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-18:00, Sa 10:00-14:00","w":"https://www.wellsfargo.com/","ph":"+1 503 3258857","ad":"1218 Commercial Street"},{"n":"Wells Fargo","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":46.189393,"lo":-123.829772,"d":1557},{"n":"Paramount Drug Co.","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":46.188871,"lo":-123.829184,"d":1602,"ad":"1319 Commercial Street"},{"n":"Columbia Memorial Hospital","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":46.187884,"lo":-123.825196,"d":1913},{"n":"Dora Badollet Library","g":"essentials","k":"library","la":46.183505,"lo":-123.823924,"d":2098,"w":"http://lrc.clatsopcc.edu/","wifi":1},{"n":"CMH-OHSU Cancer Collaborative","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":46.188204,"lo":-123.821096,"d":2226},{"n":"Columbia Memorial Hospital","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":46.187802,"lo":-123.818863,"d":2400,"h":"24/7","w":"https://www.columbiamemorial.org/","ph":"+1 503 325 4321","wp":"en:Columbia Memorial Hospital (Oregon)","wd":"Q20064928","ad":"2111 Exchange Street","ds":"Columbia Memorial Hospital (CMH) is a 25-bed medical facility in Astoria, Oregon. It is affiliated with the Evangelical Lutheran Church in America, Oregon Synod. The hospital has been serving families living and visiting the North Coast and Lower Columbia Region since 1880.","wpu":"Columbia Memorial Hospital (Oregon)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Former_Columbia_Hospital_on_16th_St_-_Astoria%2C_Oregon_%282012%29_4.jpg/960px-Former_Columbia_Hospital_on_16th_St_-_Astoria%2C_Oregon_%282012%29_4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Samuel Elmore Cannery","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.191667,"lo":-123.845833,"d":436,"ds":"Samuel Elmore Cannery was a U.S. National Historic Landmark in Astoria, Oregon that was designated in 1966 but was delisted in 1993. The home of \"Bumble Bee\" brand tuna, it was the longest continuously-operated salmon cannery in the United States, from its construction in 1898 until decommissioning in 1980.","wpu":"Samuel Elmore Cannery","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Samuel_Elmore_Cannery_-_OR_-_NHL.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Garden of Surging Waves","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.188056,"lo":-123.831389,"d":1436,"ds":"The Garden of Surging Waves (simplified Chinese: 沧浪园; traditional Chinese: 滄浪園; pinyin: Cānglàng Yuán; Jyutping: Cong1long4 Jyun4), is a Chinese garden and city park currently under construction in Astoria, Oregon. The Astoria City Council selected the garden as city's bicentennial legacy gift in recognition of Astoria's Chinese heritage.","wpu":"Garden of Surging Waves","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Garden_of_Surging_Waves%2C_Astoria.jpg/960px-Garden_of_Surging_Waves%2C_Astoria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Grace Episcopal Church (Astoria, Oregon)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.186858,"lo":-123.826297,"d":1840,"ds":"Grace Episcopal Church, also referred to as Grace Episcopal Church and Rectory in the context of historic preservation, is a church, historic church building, and accompanying parsonage, all located in Astoria, Oregon, United States. The church and rectory were listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1984.","wpu":"Grace Episcopal Church (Astoria, Oregon)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Grace_Episcopal_Church_and_Rectory_-_Astoria%2C_Oregon.jpg/960px-Grace_Episcopal_Church_and_Rectory_-_Astoria%2C_Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Youngs Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.174828,"lo":-123.865976,"d":1999,"ds":"Youngs Bay, or Youngs River Bay, is located in the northwestern corner of the U.S. state of Oregon. The Youngs River meets the Columbia River at this point, which is situated between Astoria and Warrenton. The bay is named for the Youngs River, which was discovered in 1792 by William Robert Broughton of the Vancouver Expedition.","wpu":"Youngs Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Remains_of_T_J_Potter_on_northeast_shore_of_Youngs_Bay%2C_Astoria%2C_OR%2C_2012.jpg/960px-Remains_of_T_J_Potter_on_northeast_shore_of_Youngs_Bay%2C_Astoria%2C_OR%2C_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Old Youngs Bay Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.168795,"lo":-123.838133,"d":2425,"ds":"The Old Youngs Bay Bridge is a bascule bridge across Youngs Bay in Astoria, Oregon, completed in 1921. Conde McCullough was responsible for designing this bridge, his first for Oregon. The bridge consists of two 75-foot (23 m) steel bascule leaves approached over a pile trestle and timber spans.","wpu":"Old Youngs Bay Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Old_Youngs_Bay_Bridge_bascule_section.jpg/960px-Old_Youngs_Bay_Bridge_bascule_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lewis and Clark River","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.168333,"lo":-123.863056,"d":2508,"ds":"The Lewis and Clark River is a tributary of Youngs River, approximately 20 miles (32 km) long, in northwest Oregon in the United States. It drains 62 square miles (160 km2) of the Northern Oregon Coast Range in the extreme northwest corner of the state, entering Youngs River just above its mouth on the Columbia River at Youngs Bay.","wpu":"Lewis and Clark River","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Lewis-and-clark-river.jpg/960px-Lewis-and-clark-river.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Astoria–Megler Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.217,"lo":-123.863,"d":3270,"ds":"The Astoria–Megler Bridge is a steel cantilever through-truss bridge in the Pacific Northwest region of the United States that spans the lower Columbia River. It carries a section of U.S. Route 101 from Astoria, Oregon, to Point Ellice near Megler, Washington. Opened in 1966, it is the longest continuous truss bridge in North America, and the second-longest in the world.","wpu":"Astoria–Megler Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Astoria-Megler_Bridge.jpg/960px-Astoria-Megler_Bridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lewis and Clark River Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.15229,"lo":-123.85937,"d":4145,"ds":"The Lewis and Clark River Bridge is a bascule bridge that spans the Lewis and Clark River on U.S. Route 101 Business (a section originally part of U.S. Route 101) in Clatsop County, Oregon. It was designed by Conde McCullough and opened in 1925. It was built to replace an earlier bridge at the same location, a swing-span bridge constructed around 1910.","wpu":"Lewis and Clark River Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Lewis_%26_Clark_River_Bridge_from_SE_in_2026.jpg/960px-Lewis_%26_Clark_River_Bridge_from_SE_in_2026.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lewis and Clark National Historical Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.133611,"lo":-123.8775,"d":6512,"ds":"The Lewis and Clark National Historical Park (including the former Fort Clatsop National Memorial), located in the vicinity of the mouth of the Columbia River, commemorates the Lewis and Clark Expedition. Administration of the park, which includes both federal and state lands, is a cooperative effort of the National Park Service and the states of Oregon and Washington.","wpu":"Lewis and Clark National Historical Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Fort_Clatsop_replica_2007.jpg/960px-Fort_Clatsop_replica_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"McGowan, Washington","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.246111,"lo":-123.9075,"d":7739,"ds":"McGowan was a stop on the Ilwaco Railway and Navigation Company's narrow gauge line that ran on the Long Beach Peninsula in Pacific County, Washington, United States from 1889 to 1930. In the late 19th century, P.J. McGowan bought land in the area for $1,200, and built his house, a dock and a salmon cannery on the site. During the railroad times, the main line and a passing siding ran through McGowan.","wpu":"McGowan, Washington","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/St._Mary%27s_Catholic_Church_%28a%29_McGowan%2C_Washington.jpg/960px-St._Mary%27s_Catholic_Church_%28a%29_McGowan%2C_Washington.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Stevens (Oregon)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":46.201944,"lo":-123.9625,"d":8777,"ds":"Fort Stevens was an American military installation that guarded the mouth of the Columbia River in the state of Oregon. Built near the end of the American Civil War, it was named for Civil War general and former Washington Territory governor Isaac I. Stevens. The fort was an active military reservation from 1863–1947.","wpu":"Fort Stevens (Oregon)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Fort_Stevens_Oregon.JPG/960px-Fort_Stevens_Oregon.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Astoria is a port city in and the county seat of Clatsop County, Oregon, United States. The population was 10,181 at the 2020 census. Founded in 1811, Astoria is the oldest city in the state and was the first permanent American settlement west of the Rocky Mountains.","wpu":"Astoria, Oregon","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/Astoria_Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}